gap:
Principles and some measured numbers are good: 3.7 MB to 532 KB mostly by resizing textures, a 7 KB Meshopt decoder vs 73 KB for Draco, a GLB of about 1.5 MB or less, texture caps, a benchmark harness, baked AO and accumulating frames while the camera rests. The practical steps are missing:
- no gltf-transform commands (optimize, resize, KTX2 texture compression, dedup, prune, instance);
- no KTX2 guidance on UASTC vs ETC1S (normal maps vs albedo) or on colour space per map;
- a headless Blender bake is recommended but never shown, and there are no export settings;
- no instancing (InstancedMesh or EXT_mesh_gpu_instancing) and no draw-call target (draw calls are only "recorded");
- no lightmap or UV2 baking, and no LOD switch thresholds;
- the "build script that fails" when an asset is over budget is described but not shipped.
KTX2 and Blender baking have no lesson row or measured result; WebP is the only path that was actually tried.

The motion judgement is senior-grade:
- one named job per animation;
- a frequency gate with numbers (100+ a day or keyboard-triggered means none);
- duration and easing tokens, and springs with stiffness and damping;
- interruption rules (transitions retarget, keyframes restart, springs keep velocity);
- stagger capped at 30–80 ms with a 300 ms total.
Missing:
- durations scaled by distance, size and device (shorter on phones, longer for large travel);
- a choreography vocabulary beyond stagger (lead and follow, shared axis, container transform, fade through);
- classical principles for expressive moments (anticipation, follow-through, overlapping action);
- a motion-study step (a quick prototype or filmstrip reviewed before building) instead of deciding in prose.

The skill knows Rive's weight (56 KB JS plus 787 KB WASM), the MIT runtime licence, the paid editor export, and that loops pause under reduced motion. It does not know:
- when Rive is worth it: state machines driven by inputs (interactive characters, stateful toggles, onboarding mascots, data-bound UI), with one runtime shared across many assets;
- the lighter builds (@rive-app/canvas-lite) or WebGL2 vs canvas renderers;
- a poster or first-frame strategy while the WASM loads;
- how to expose Rive inputs to keyboard and screen readers, since it is a canvas;
- the designer-to-developer contract (artboard and state-machine input names).
Interactive SVG as the cheaper alternative (SVG with CSS or WAAPI states, GSAP MorphSVG) is absent. The S2 lab's Rive vs Lottie vs SVG/CSS toggle comparison is not folded in.

The skill gates shaders well (the decoration list, OGL for "one shader plane", the licence trap). It also has one strong performance method: four flag combinations isolated the MSAA and AO cost. It has nothing on making shaders:
- GLSL, TSL or WGSL basics;
- noise families (value, simplex, curl, FBM) and which reads as which material;
- GPU particles (points, instancing, compute);
- post-processing stacks (pmndrs postprocessing, tone mapping, colour management, bloom thresholds);
- shader compile hitches and pre-warming (renderer.compile);
- mobile precision;
- static and reduced-motion fallbacks for generative backgrounds;
- seeded, deterministic output so the server poster matches the client.

There is no map of where creative developers learn and publish (Codrops, the three.js forum and Journey, pmndrs examples, Shadertoy, CodePen, OpenProcessing, Awwwards case studies, GDC game-feel talks). There is no procedure for turning a demo into production:
- check the licence (Codrops demo terms, CodePen's MIT default, Shadertoy's NC);
- strip the demo's assumptions (desktop GPU, fixed size, no resize, no reduced motion, no accessibility, no loading or error states, global singletons);
- budget it and test it on a mid-range phone.
The only demo-vs-production judgement in the skill is Spline, "prototypes only" at 1,062 KB.

Nothing on sound as feedback or brand:
- earcons on placement and success, volume and mixing;
- off by default with a visible, persistent toggle, and system mute respected;
- Web Audio unlocked on a user gesture, with one shared AudioContext;
- synthesis vs files and library cost (Howler about 8 KB, zzfx about 1 KB, Tone about 60 KB or more);
- a visual equivalent for any meaning carried by sound.
Nothing on haptics either: navigator.vibrate is unsupported on iOS Safari, patterns must stay short, and vibration is never the only feedback. The repo's S6 lab measured the library payloads and vibrate support (research/stage2/experiments/S6-interactive-experiences/results/audio.json), but none of it reaches the skill.

The skill covers viewers (a name, rotate and zoom buttons, reduced motion, touch-action: pan-y, a poster fallback) and single-pointer alternatives to drags. It does not cover a canvas people build on:
- role="img" is the wrong pattern for a builder, because it hides every object and action;
- no DOM proxy layer (a focusable element per scene object, kept in sync with its screen box);
- no keyboard verbs for select, move, rotate and delete;
- no live-region narration of scene changes;
- no equivalent non-canvas path (a form that produces the same spec);
- no game-accessibility practice (Game Accessibility Guidelines, Xbox XAGs: remapping, hold vs toggle, timing, colour-blind modes).
The tools cannot see inside the canvas. a11y.mjs and widgets.mjs treat it as one element, motion.mjs says canvas frames are invisible, and audit.mjs's unlabelled-canvas signal pushes toward role="img". In the unused S6 lab, the canvas-only variant completed 0 of 3 keyboard tasks.

The budgets are specific: novelty per category, two or three brand moments, the frequency gate, "one stage per page", and a rule that spectacle must improve understanding, confidence, sharing or ordering. Missing are the craft of the moment itself and a way to fail it:
- delight decays with repetition, so return visits need a shorter or skippable version;
- the point where a moment becomes friction (it delays input, blocks the next action, or costs INP);
- easter eggs and discoverable depth;
- an interaction signature as brand: a characteristic spring, cursor, sound or feedback voice (Linear, Stripe, Duolingo);
- measures such as share or screen-record rate, return rate, and task time unchanged.
The critique template has no row that tests delight or its cost.

Six rules backed by evidence, plus a scripted gesture test: don't move the canvas under the pointer; a tap-vs-orbit threshold; one history entry per gesture; a ghost for dragging; a single-pointer alternative; a beautiful default. Absent:
- a layout model for canvas, inspector and toolbar (persistent vs contextual toolbars; where tools live on a phone vs a desktop);
- a layers panel and z-order; multi-select by shift-click and marquee; transform handles;
- snapping and smart guides with thresholds; arrow-key nudge (1 px / 10 px);
- the standard shortcut map (undo/redo, Delete, duplicate, copy/paste, space-drag pan, pinch or ⌘-scroll zoom, zoom to fit);
- keyboard access to canvas objects;
- autosave with a visible status, version history, and conflicts across two tabs.
The only save rule, 'never mix auto-save and explicit save in one form', is written for forms.
